<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#el-nino">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">El Niño</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as El Niño–Southern Oscillation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">El Niño is the periodic warming of surface waters in the central and eastern tropical Pacific, part of a climate cycle that recurs every few years. On the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> it brings torrential rains and floods to normally dry deserts and drives away fish from cold upwelling waters. Strong El Niño events have been linked to crises in ancient Andean societies, including the disruption of the Moche in the 6th century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-nino.jpg" alt="El Niño" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Content published by Rebecca Lindsey and reviewed by Tom Di Liberto. Image credit to NOAA Climate.gov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NOAA_Nino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
